ory, to justify the added complexity and risk.\n\nThe user is benchmarking against OmniFunds' +86% YTD and 10-11%/month figures, so they clearly want a high performance bar. Given the packet explicitly says the criterion must be strong enough that ~8-10% CAGR with ~20% MDD clearly fails, the threshold at that drawdown level needs to sit well above 10%.\n\nI'm thinking the scaling should tie required CAGR to MDD bands — maybe 15% at 20% MDD, scaling up to 25%+ at 40% MDD, roughly targeting Calmar ratios near 0.75. But these numbers shouldn't feel arbitrary; they need grounding in first principles, like outperforming passive equity benchmarks (SPY's ~10-11% CAGR with ~55% MDD) and the best simple timing strategies, with enough margin to survive the typical backtest-to-live performance decay.\n\nI'm recalling research showing out-of-sample return decay around 26% and post-publication decay around 58% for factor anomalies, plus accounting for multiple-testing bias since the GA tested roughly 130k unique genomes, which inflates the apparent best performer. So I'm building two criteria: first, a dominance test where blind CAGR must beat the passive and timing baselines by a margin exceeding the estimated selection-bias haircut, and second, an absolute floor — minimum CAGR requirements that rise with each MDD band.\n\nI'm testing a scaling formula tying minimum CAGR to the MDD band, but running into tension at the low-risk 10% band: a 7.5% CAGR with ≤10% MDD gives a strong Calmar ratio of 0.75-0.8, which is clearly a good low-risk product even if it doesn't satisfy the more aggressive wealth-growth objective. Since the overall design is meant to be a Pareto frontier letting users choose their own risk band, I'm deciding the floor at the 10% band just needs to clear T-bill returns by a meaningful margin rather than match the stricter thresholds used for higher-risk bands.\n\nLooking at the 40% band, the observed 16.55% CAGR was flagged as \"economically unacceptable\" relative to the project's goals, which implies the true floor there needs to be notably higher -- but I want to avoid just reverse-engineering a threshold to reject that one data point. Instead I'm reasoning from first principles: passive equities accept roughly -55% drawdowns for ~10-11% CAGR, so a system willingly taking on 40% drawdown should be expected to deliver substantially more than that passive benchmark, perhaps on the order of double the CAGR, to justify the added risk. I'm now considering defining the criteria as excess returns over SPY/T-bill benchmarks measured on the identical time window, so the comparison stays apples-to-apples.\n\nI'm sketching concrete floors: matched-risk dominance requiring blind CAGR to exceed the best passive comparator at the same max drawdown by several points per year, plus an absolute floor like CAGR ≥ SPY total return CAGR + 5 points at bands ≥25%, and +3 points at the 20% band, which given historical SPY CAGR of roughly 10-11% would translate into something like 13-14% minimum at the 20% band. I'm also weighing how much margin to add for the expected performance decay between development and live validation, since excess returns typically shrink 30-50% out of sample, and thinking about laying this out as a two-tier table of minimum-to-pass versus target thresholds.\n\nNow I'm trying to formalize the economic floor per drawdown band, proposing that CAGR exceed the risk-free rate plus a multiple of the drawdown band (effectively requiring a Calmar ratio of at least 1.0 on an excess-over-cash basis), and I'm starting to estimate what the average 3-month T-bill rate was across 2006-2026 to anchor that risk-free baseline.\n\nBut applying a straight linear Calmar≥1 rule across all bands produces unrealistic targets at the high-tolerance end -- a 40% drawdown band would demand over 41% CAGR, which no long-run systematic equity strategy actually achieves, so I'm considering a sublinear scaling instead and trying to recall what the best documented long-term systematic strategies have actually delivered.\n\nLooking at real-world benchmarks, the best long-only momentum or trend-timing strategies on large-cap US equities tend to land around 15-20% CAGR with 25-35% max drawdown, giving Calmar ratios near 0.5-0.7 even in favorable backtests -- so a true Calmar≥1 over a 20-year span including 2008 is genuinely rare. Since the user's 8-10% CAGR at 20% drawdown (Calmar ~0.45) needs to clearly fail my bar, I'm leaning toward setting the 20%-band floor around 15% CAGR, which gives Calmar ~0.7 and fails that example while still being grounded in the idea that returns must meaningfully exceed passive equity benchmarks rather than being reverse-engineered from the specific number given.\n\nI'm trying to formalize this as a rule where the minimum required CAGR is the larger of (benchmark SPY CAGR plus a drawdown-scaled margin) or (the best comparable passive/timing strategy's CAGR at matched drawdown, plus a 5-point buffer for strategy decay). It's getting complex, so I want to simplify it into a clean, testable table covering the full 20-year blind run.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1vcHVzLTUuNS0yMDI2MDkyMXxjbGF1ZGUtb24tYXdzIn0"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":9758,"completion_tokens":32000,"total_tokens":41758,"cost":0.679032,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.679032,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.039032,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.64},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":32000,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}